---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const withBase = (p = '') => (base.endsWith('/') ? base : base + '/') + p;
---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about depression and despair"
  description="The Bhagavad Gita opens in despair — its first chapter is named for Arjuna's collapse. Here is what it offers when you feel low, hopeless or unable to go on, and the verses that speak to it."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-depression"
  datePublished="2026-10-17"
  faq={[
    {
      q: 'What does the Bhagavad Gita say about depression?',
      a: 'The Gita opens with despair: its first chapter depicts Arjuna collapsing, unable to act. It does not shame that state — it answers it, counselling that the self can lift itself (6.5), that painful states pass (2.14), and that one need not carry everything alone (18.66). Note the Gita describes spiritual despondency, not clinical depression, which deserves professional care.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which Bhagavad Gita verse helps with feeling low or hopeless?',
      a: 'Verse 6.5 — "lift yourself by your own self; do not degrade yourself" — is the most direct. Verse 2.14 counsels that pleasant and painful states are impermanent and can be endured, and 18.66 offers the relief of setting the burden down.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is the first chapter of the Gita about depression?',
      a: 'Chapter 1 is traditionally titled Arjuna Vishada Yoga — "the yoga of Arjuna’s dejection." The entire teaching that follows is Krishna’s response to a man who has sunk down, overwhelmed and unwilling to go on.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can the Bhagavad Gita cure depression?',
      a: 'No scripture is a treatment for clinical depression, and the Gita does not claim to be one. What it offers is meaning, perspective and a way to begin moving again. If low mood is persistent or severe, please treat it as you would any illness and seek professional help.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The Bhagavad Gita is often remembered as a book about action, but it begins with a man who cannot
    act at all. Chapter 1 is called <em>Arjuna Vishada Yoga</em> — "the yoga of Arjuna's dejection."
    Its hero sinks down in his chariot, lets his weapon fall, and says he would rather not go on.
    Before a word of teaching, the Gita gives despair its own chapter.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters. Whatever brought you to search for this, the text does not treat your low place as a
    failure to be scolded away. It treats it as the starting point — the exact condition the rest of
    the Gita was spoken to meet.
  </p>

  <p>
    One honest thing first: the Gita describes a <em>spiritual</em> despondency — the heaviness of
    meaning lost, of not knowing why to rise. Clinical depression is an illness, and it deserves care
    like any other. If what you feel is persistent, crushing, or pulling you toward harming yourself,
    please read the note near the end of this page before anything else.
  </p>

  <h2>Be your own friend, not your own enemy</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's counsel for a sunken mind is strikingly modern: the lift has to begin from within, and
    the way you treat yourself decides whether you rise or sink further.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Let a person lift themselves by their own self; let them not degrade themselves. For the self is the friend of the self, and also its enemy."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/6/5')}>Bhagavad Gita 6.5</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Notice it does not say "snap out of it." It names a choice you make toward yourself — to be, in a
    low hour, a friend rather than a prosecutor. On the days when everything is heavy, becoming your
    own enemy is the one weight you can choose to set down.
  </p>

  <h2>This state, too, is passing</h2>
  <p>
    Depression lies by insisting the present will be permanent. The Gita answers that nothing in the
    realm of feeling stays.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"The contacts of the senses give cold and heat, pleasure and pain. They come and go, impermanent. Endure them, Arjuna."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/14')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.14</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    "Endure" here is not grim teeth-gritting. It is the quiet knowledge that this weather, however
    total it feels, is weather — that it moves, and that you do not have to believe its claim to be
    forever.
  </p>

  <h2>Begin with the next small thing</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's cure for paralysis is not a grand resolution but a return to small, doable action —
    done without demanding that it fix everything at once.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Your right is to action alone, never to its fruits."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/47')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.47</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    When the weight of the whole outcome is what pins you down, this verse narrows the task to the only
    part that was ever yours: the next honest action, released from the demand that it rescue you. Make
    the tea. Answer the one message. The fruit is not your burden today.
  </p>

  <h2>You were not meant to carry it alone</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Abandon all other supports and take refuge in me alone. I will free you from all that binds you; do not grieve."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/18/66')}>Bhagavad Gita 18.66</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Read it devotionally or simply as permission — either way it ends the Gita on the same note it
    began answering: you do not have to hold all of this by yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Please read this if the low is heavy</h2>
  <p>
    A text can offer meaning, but it cannot replace a person or a doctor. If your low mood is
    persistent or severe, treat it as the real health matter it is and reach out — to someone you
    trust, a GP, or a mental-health professional. If you are thinking of harming yourself, please
    contact a crisis line now: in India, <strong>KIRAN</strong> (1800-599-0019) and
    <strong>iCall</strong> (9152987821) are free and confidential. The Gita will keep. You matter more.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to read next</h2>
  <p>
    The movement from despair to action runs from <a href={withBase('gita/1')}>Chapter 1</a> into
    <a href={withBase('gita/2')}>Chapter 2</a> — reading them in order is itself the arc out of
    dejection. If the heaviness is tangled with loss, see
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-death-and-grief')}>the Gita on death and grief</a>; if
    with dread, <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-fear')}>the Gita on fear</a>. Or open Gita
    Daily and let a verse meet you where you are today.
  </p>
</Guide>
